\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Opus Feedback:\
Agreed, and the mixed results are where the good questions are. Here's what I'd want to chase, starting with my favourite:\uc0\u8232 \u8232 1. **Laddered expiries (my pick).** The biggest surprise today was how much protection depended on where the expiry date fell, with long puts' validation benefit ranging from 10.35 to 2.23 points on window placement alone. So instead of one quarterly expiry, split the same budget across three staggered tranches. If that makes results steadier without costing much protection, it's a real structural idea rather than a parameter tweak. It needs dated option entry in `replay`, which is already on the request list.\u8232 \u8232 2. **When to cash in.** Every option here is held to expiry, even though much of the value in a crash exists at the low and can fade by expiry. A simple rule like "sell after a large gain and reset the strike" might matter more than which strike you buy. I'd start by measuring how much payoff at the low was lost by expiry, which is cheap to do.\u8232 \u8232 3. **A small real-quote reality check.** This is the traditional one, and probably the most informative per hour once you have data. Even a few dozen real option prices around calm and stressed dates would show how far Black-Scholes at trailing volatility is off, especially for out-of-the-money puts. It would tell us which Stage 6 conclusions survive.\u8232 \u8232 4. **Hedging what the book actually loses on.** In the worst test window the book lost 10.05% while XLF fell only 2.3%, so a sector hedge may be aimed at the wrong risk. The book is really a bet on banks against capital-markets names. I'd like to see how much of the tail is that spread, and whether a regional-bank ETF or a basket hedge fits it better. That first number is only a quick check so far.\u8232 \u8232 5. **The orthogonal one: a crisis simulator.** We only have a handful of real crises, and no amount of bootstrapping creates more. The Stage 1 simulation could be extended with jumps and volatility spikes, then used to run these overlays through thousands of synthetic crashes of different speeds. It wouldn't prove anything about real markets, but it could show which kinds of crash each hedge handles, which 23 windows can't. That would make crash speed and shape something we can study directly, and it would turn "options only pay in fast crashes" from a phrase we had to retract into a question we can actually test.\u8232 \u8232 If I could only do one, it'd be the ladder, with the quick measurement from #2 alongside it, since both come from the same finding: timing mattered more than we designed for. Since 2021\'962026 is already used up, I'd freeze a new protocol first and label any work on those dates as retrospective.\
>>>>>>>>>>\
The real-quote reality check (#3). The ladder is the more interesting idea, but every overlay conclusion so far rests on Black-Scholes at trailing volatility, including whether a ladder would even be worth its cost.\uc0\u8232 \u8232 We saw how much that matters: at 1.5\'d7 pricing volatility, the long-put benefit disappears. A few dozen real quotes around calm and stressed dates would show roughly where actual prices sit against that range, especially for out-of-the-money puts, where skew should hurt the model most. It would also show whether the collar stays affordable, since real skew makes the puts dearer and the calls cheaper.\u8232 \u8232 It's also a small job for what it tells us. The harder part is sourcing the data, because no options source has been acquired yet. If it can't be sourced soon, I'd do the cash-in measurement (#2) next, since it only needs the data we already have.\
